1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,000
Born in fiery furnaces.

2
00:00:03,001 --> 00:00:08,000
It's the miracle material of spaceships and soda cans.

3
00:00:08,001 --> 00:00:12,000
Light enough to fly, but tough enough to hit a baseball.

4
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:18,000
It can protect and preserve, or reflect and reveal the secrets of the universe.

5
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Stamped, melted, rolled, or even foamed, it has changed the world.

6
00:00:25,001 --> 00:00:29,000
Now, Aluminum on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
There's a good reason the world devours nearly 100,000 tons of aluminum every day.

8
00:00:50,600 --> 00:00:55,060
And why the United States alone gobbles 25 billion pounds a year.

9
00:00:55,061 --> 00:01:02,060
Modern civilization couldn't survive without it.

10
00:01:02,061 --> 00:01:08,060
This strong, lightweight, flexible, rust-proof wonder is everywhere.

11
00:01:08,061 --> 00:01:13,060
An integral part of the planes we fly, the cars we drive,

12
00:01:13,061 --> 00:01:16,060
the structures of our cities,

13
00:01:16,061 --> 00:01:20,060
and the power lines carrying electricity to our homes.

14
00:01:20,061 --> 00:01:27,060
But most know it best when aluminum is no thicker than a human hair.

15
00:01:27,061 --> 00:01:35,060
It's found this way in over 95% of households, as aluminum foil.

16
00:01:35,061 --> 00:01:43,060
Impervious to light, air and moisture, it's perfect for preparing and preserving food.

17
00:01:43,061 --> 00:01:46,060
There's nothing better for a barrier.

18
00:01:46,061 --> 00:01:54,460
Moisture protection, freshness, the deadfold characteristics that we use to wrap our products is just, there's nothing better.

19
00:01:55,780 --> 00:01:57,680
But how does it get so thin?

20
00:01:58,220 --> 00:02:01,300
And why is it shiny on one side and dull on the other?

21
00:02:03,100 --> 00:02:11,860
The Reynolds factory in Richmond, Virginia, maker of 340,000 pounds of aluminum foil every day, holds the answers.

22
00:02:11,861 --> 00:02:20,860
The aluminum arrives by rail car as massive 32,000 pound coils.

23
00:02:20,861 --> 00:02:28,860
Once we receive the coils, they're unloaded off the rail car, we go into annealing ovens.

24
00:02:28,861 --> 00:02:34,860
The annealing process helps soften the metal so that we can further reduce it to the gauge

25
00:02:34,861 --> 00:02:39,860
and thickness that most people see in our standard Reynolds-wrapped products.

26
00:02:39,861 --> 00:02:44,080
Each sheet is 4 1⁄2 thousandths of an inch thick,

27
00:02:44,081 --> 00:02:45,880
about the same as a compact disc.

28
00:02:49,140 --> 00:02:51,920
Thinning the softened sheet is a job for the rolling mills.

29
00:02:53,380 --> 00:02:55,300
Since the pressure they exert on the aluminum

30
00:02:55,301 --> 00:02:57,800
could create sparks and ignite fires,

31
00:02:59,580 --> 00:03:02,580
a cooling lubricant douses the sheet as it enters.

32
00:03:04,520 --> 00:03:07,280
Inside the mill, the sheet passes between

33
00:03:07,281 --> 00:03:09,783
pair of powered spinning rollers.

34
00:03:09,784 --> 00:03:13,880
Larger, non-powered rollers positioned above and below

35
00:03:13,881 --> 00:03:20,388
them apply even pressure, helping them flatten the sheet in a uniform manner.

36
00:03:20,389 --> 00:03:21,740
One pass through

37
00:03:21,741 --> 00:03:26,114
the mill reduces the sheet thickness by half.

38
00:03:26,115 --> 00:03:30,780
It's also getting a lot longer as the 3,000-yard

39
00:03:30,781 --> 00:03:36,103
long coiled sheet makes six passes through three different rolling mills.

40
00:03:36,104 --> 00:03:37,780
When it finishes on our

41
00:03:37,781 --> 00:03:43,200
finishing mill, it's about 240 miles long because we've reduced it and increased the length.

42
00:03:45,140 --> 00:03:50,200
This final pass through the finishing mill holds the secret to why aluminum foil is shiny on one

43
00:03:50,201 --> 00:03:51,589
side, but not the other.

44
00:03:51,590 --> 00:03:55,640
So in order to support the metal and ensure we can pull it through the

45
00:03:55,641 --> 00:03:56,763
mill, we double pass it.

46
00:03:56,764 --> 00:04:00,040
We have two sheets of metal running through the mill at the same time.

47
00:04:00,780 --> 00:04:07,780
Since the outer sides of each sheet contact the highly polished lubricated roller surface,

48
00:04:07,781 --> 00:04:09,780
they emerge shiny.

49
00:04:09,781 --> 00:04:20,780
And since the inner sides touch only each other, they emerge with a matte finish.

50
00:04:20,781 --> 00:04:26,780
After the doubled foil exits, each single layer is only 6.4 ten-thousandths of an inch thick,

51
00:04:26,781 --> 00:04:30,780
six times thinner than a sheet of standard copier paper.

52
00:04:30,781 --> 00:04:35,780
In this process, we're separating the metal from the double sheet to single sheet.

53
00:04:35,781 --> 00:04:38,780
The separation occurs at this point.

54
00:04:38,781 --> 00:04:42,780
One sheet is going to the upper roll, the other sheet to the lower roll.

55
00:04:43,780 --> 00:04:45,780
As the sheets of foil separate,

56
00:04:45,781 --> 00:04:48,780
a set of knives behind this metal housing

57
00:04:48,781 --> 00:04:51,780
slices the roll into six equal divisions,

58
00:04:51,781 --> 00:04:53,780
measuring 12 inches across.

59
00:04:56,780 --> 00:05:01,780
Next, mechanical arms break apart the divisions.

60
00:05:01,781 --> 00:05:05,780
Now the foil is at its final thickness and width.

61
00:05:08,780 --> 00:05:14,780
Shortening each coil to the standard length happens at the packing facility.

62
00:05:15,780 --> 00:05:23,780
The coils run through a spooler that cuts each sheet and wraps it around a fiber core 300 times per minute.

63
00:05:26,780 --> 00:05:29,780
Machines then guide the cores into cartons,

64
00:05:29,781 --> 00:05:34,780
and the foil is ready to wrap food for the fridge, freezer, or grill all around the world.

65
00:05:34,781 --> 00:05:38,780
But does it matter which side faces in and which side faces out?

66
00:05:38,781 --> 00:05:44,780
Actually, it makes no difference whether you use the dull versus the shiny side of the foil.

67
00:05:44,781 --> 00:05:49,780
They both are going to perform the same for cooking or freezing or storing with foil.

68
00:05:50,780 --> 00:05:55,780
Aluminum foil's roots stretch back over eight decades.

69
00:05:56,780 --> 00:06:02,120
When metals other than aluminum were preserving the goods that fed our sweet tooths and smoking habits.

70
00:06:03,820 --> 00:06:14,300
It got its origins way back in about the 1920s when Reynolds Metals Company was in the business of producing lead and tin foil that was used in cigarette packaging.

71
00:06:14,860 --> 00:06:23,260
And they started wondering if possibly this protective foil could be made better and more economically by using the new metal called aluminum.

72
00:06:23,261 --> 00:06:28,260
Aluminum arrived for home use in 1947.

73
00:06:28,261 --> 00:06:35,940
Touted as a new kitchen miracle, Reynolds Wrap debuted as a 25-foot long, 12-inch wide

74
00:06:35,941 --> 00:06:39,260
roll, packaged in pink and silver.

75
00:06:39,261 --> 00:06:45,380
Well, aluminum foil as a household foil was never made from lead or tin.

76
00:06:45,381 --> 00:06:50,260
And it's kind of interesting that people still refer to it sometimes as tin foil today.

77
00:06:50,261 --> 00:06:58,820
But where does the aluminum used to make foil and a million other things come from?

78
00:06:58,821 --> 00:07:03,820
It's right beneath our feet, beckoning to miners as the most abundant metal in the Earth's

79
00:07:03,821 --> 00:07:05,820
crust.

80
00:07:05,821 --> 00:07:12,860
Out of every one million atoms in the ground, 82,000 are aluminum.

81
00:07:12,861 --> 00:07:17,380
But pure aluminum doesn't exist on its own in nature.

82
00:07:17,381 --> 00:07:23,460
can be found locked away in reddish deposits of bauxite ore.

83
00:07:23,461 --> 00:07:35,960
The richest deposits of the ore exist in Australia, Guinea, Brazil, and Jamaica.

84
00:07:35,961 --> 00:07:41,920
A refining process will begin to extract the stubborn aluminum.

85
00:07:41,921 --> 00:07:46,240
A caustic soda digests the ore-rich dirt into a liquid

86
00:07:46,241 --> 00:07:50,020
and allows the separation of its components.

87
00:07:50,021 --> 00:07:52,180
The resulting white residue is then

88
00:07:52,181 --> 00:07:57,100
dried into a fine white powder, a compound called aluminum

89
00:07:57,101 --> 00:07:59,160
oxide or aluminum.

90
00:08:01,900 --> 00:08:05,800
This is the stuff from which aluminum is made.

91
00:08:05,801 --> 00:08:09,020
The trick now is isolating the aluminum from the oxygen.

92
00:08:11,920 --> 00:08:16,340
To pull that off, you need to transport the alumina to a production facility,

93
00:08:16,720 --> 00:08:20,340
like the one Alcoa operates here, near Evansville, Indiana.

94
00:08:22,020 --> 00:08:25,260
We receive a barge every day into our oar dock.

95
00:08:25,620 --> 00:08:31,060
It's got roughly 3.2 million pounds of alumina on it, and we vacuum it out of the barge.

96
00:08:31,120 --> 00:08:33,880
It's basically like a large vacuum cleaner that you'd use at home.

97
00:08:35,500 --> 00:08:38,600
The vacuum snorts about 300 tons per hour,

98
00:08:38,601 --> 00:08:41,600
emptying the barge in 10 hours

99
00:08:41,601 --> 00:08:44,920
and depositing the powder onto conveyor belts.

100
00:08:46,120 --> 00:08:48,260
Actually, there's no smell to it at all.

101
00:08:48,860 --> 00:08:50,860
It's got a real granular feel to it.

102
00:08:51,020 --> 00:08:54,800
It's a little more coarse than what talcum powder is,

103
00:08:54,801 --> 00:08:56,600
more like salt.

104
00:08:58,720 --> 00:09:01,760
Inside the 120-acre mega-complex,

105
00:09:02,520 --> 00:09:04,960
750 pots await the powder.

106
00:09:04,961 --> 00:09:10,880
Each contains a molten salt bath.

107
00:09:10,881 --> 00:09:17,520
Every two minutes, an overhead feeder dumps seven pounds of alumina into one of the pots.

108
00:09:17,521 --> 00:09:28,440
As the powder begins to melt, the most critical component to isolating the aluminum takes charge.

109
00:09:28,441 --> 00:09:31,840
Electricity, and lots of it.

110
00:09:31,841 --> 00:09:40,380
The on-site coal plant delivers enough juice to the pots to power 300,000 homes.

111
00:09:40,381 --> 00:09:45,340
The electricity flows through copper rods connected to steel blocks suspended in the

112
00:09:45,341 --> 00:09:51,200
pot's salt bath.

113
00:09:51,201 --> 00:09:57,560
The aluminum oxide molecules filling the bath contain two aluminum atoms and three oxygen

114
00:09:57,561 --> 00:09:59,460
atoms.

115
00:09:59,461 --> 00:10:03,460
The electric current breaks the bond and forces the atoms apart.

116
00:10:03,461 --> 00:10:08,460
And in that process you're making liquid metal and the metal is a little bit denser than the bath.

117
00:10:08,461 --> 00:10:13,460
It settles to the bottom of the pot and then carbon dioxide bubbles are given off.

118
00:10:13,461 --> 00:10:16,460
But the process isn't over yet.

119
00:10:16,461 --> 00:10:22,460
Workers then have to extract the molten aluminum from the pots and turn it into a solid.

120
00:10:22,461 --> 00:10:30,440
Crews literally vacuum the metal out in a process we call tapping.

121
00:10:31,040 --> 00:10:35,580
They use a large container that's got a spout on it, and we apply air to it and create a

122
00:10:35,581 --> 00:10:37,480
vacuum and suck the metal out of the bottom of the pot.

123
00:10:40,360 --> 00:10:46,060
Forklifts then transport the crucibles to a holding furnace, where cranes lift and spill

124
00:10:46,061 --> 00:10:46,980
out the molten metal.

125
00:10:46,981 --> 00:10:55,580
Then, channels directed into casting chambers, where it will solidify.

126
00:10:57,920 --> 00:11:03,180
These hulking monoliths are destined to become everything from beer cans to boats,

127
00:11:04,760 --> 00:11:07,760
from saucepans to siding on your house.

128
00:11:08,780 --> 00:11:11,680
Not bad for a metal that's definitely the new kid on the block.

129
00:11:12,140 --> 00:11:16,100
Well, the one thing to keep in mind is that aluminum is a fairly new metal.

130
00:11:16,101 --> 00:11:20,940
Bronze, lead, iron, copper have been around for centuries.

131
00:11:21,620 --> 00:11:29,960
Aluminum was first actually discovered as an element by a chemist, Sir Davy, in 1808.

132
00:11:31,240 --> 00:11:33,880
But discovering this unique metal was one thing.

133
00:11:34,840 --> 00:11:37,080
Divorcing it from its ore compound was another.

134
00:11:38,380 --> 00:11:40,040
Throughout most of the 19th century,

135
00:11:40,041 --> 00:11:45,880
a variety of chemical processes could only generate impure samples in minute quantities.

136
00:11:46,100 --> 00:11:56,600
Aluminum was so rare, it was nearly twice as expensive as gold.

137
00:11:56,601 --> 00:12:02,320
In the 1880s, when advances in processing helped reduce its price, it was the fitting

138
00:12:02,321 --> 00:12:08,640
choice for engineers completing construction of the Washington Monument, who sought a precious

139
00:12:08,641 --> 00:12:11,900
capstone for their masterwork.

140
00:12:11,901 --> 00:12:18,080
The aluminum pyramid they placed atop the marble spire weighed 100 ounces, at the time

141
00:12:18,081 --> 00:12:22,560
the largest piece of aluminum ever cast.

142
00:12:22,561 --> 00:12:30,440
Two years later in 1886 came the discovery that made aluminum accessible and inexpensive.

143
00:12:30,441 --> 00:12:37,540
Two scientists working independently, Frenchman Paul Aru and American Charles Martin Hall,

144
00:12:37,541 --> 00:12:44,540
upon the chemical electric extraction process still in use today.

145
00:12:45,300 --> 00:12:50,700
Aluminum's market price plummeted to less than a dollar per pound.

146
00:12:50,701 --> 00:12:57,620
The breakthrough unshackled the metal and triggered the rise of revolutionary new industries.

147
00:12:57,621 --> 00:13:01,020
Today one of those industries crafts the aluminum

148
00:13:01,021 --> 00:13:04,300
that helps lift the most massive commercial airliner

149
00:13:04,301 --> 00:13:09,500
ever built.

150
00:13:09,501 --> 00:13:12,920
Producing new aluminum from recycled aluminum scrap

151
00:13:12,921 --> 00:13:17,960
requires just 5% of the energy needed to create it from raw materials.

152
00:13:17,961 --> 00:13:21,540
Aluminum will return on Modern Marvels

153
00:13:21,541 --> 00:13:24,540
here on the History Channel.

154
00:13:24,541 --> 00:13:30,900
We now return to aluminum on Modern Marvels.

155
00:13:30,901 --> 00:13:33,640
This Goliath double-decker jet,

156
00:13:33,641 --> 00:13:38,180
The Airbus A380 is the largest commercial aircraft in the world.

157
00:13:39,040 --> 00:13:44,060
It stretches 10 feet longer and towers 16 feet taller than a Boeing 747.

158
00:13:47,540 --> 00:13:51,180
It weighs in at a whopping 1.2 million pounds.

159
00:13:52,620 --> 00:13:58,220
And like most commercial aircraft, 65% of that bulk is aluminum.

160
00:13:58,221 --> 00:14:05,220
It's a no-brainer that aluminum's light weight makes it a perfect construction material for planes.

161
00:14:05,221 --> 00:14:09,220
But more important is its flexibility.

162
00:14:09,221 --> 00:14:13,220
Aluminum is ideally suited for flying more than any other material.

163
00:14:13,221 --> 00:14:21,220
It's important that the material be able to withstand and give and take in those pressurizations and depressurizations

164
00:14:21,221 --> 00:14:26,220
that occur thousands of times over the life cycle of an airplane.

165
00:14:28,220 --> 00:14:32,460
The wings of the A380, like any airplane, suffer constant stress.

166
00:14:35,960 --> 00:14:38,480
On the ground, they sag from their own weight.

167
00:14:40,640 --> 00:14:45,220
In the sky, the airflow required for lift pushes the wings in the opposite direction.

168
00:14:47,640 --> 00:14:55,340
The A380's pliable wings span over 260 feet and are comprised of the largest pieces of aluminum on the plane.

169
00:14:58,220 --> 00:15:04,260
Ten separate sheets case the surface of each wing, five on top and five underneath.

170
00:15:06,200 --> 00:15:12,680
They cover a series of aluminum ribs and spars, which form the skeleton needed to support the wing's length.

171
00:15:15,920 --> 00:15:25,460
The wing skins for the A380 are among the thousands of aluminum parts made here at Alcoa's Davenport Works Mill in Iowa.

172
00:15:25,461 --> 00:15:30,460
The plant is large enough to contain a golf course.

173
00:15:30,461 --> 00:15:40,460
Mill workers craft wing sections up to 112 feet long and weighing over 11,000 pounds.

174
00:15:40,461 --> 00:15:50,460
They begin with enormous 18-inch thick, 70-inch wide aluminum alloy blocks, weighing 30,000 pounds.

175
00:15:50,461 --> 00:15:59,580
The task of transforming each into a giant wing skin begins with this beastly rolling

176
00:15:59,581 --> 00:16:05,080
mill, measuring over 18 feet across, the widest in the world.

177
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:11,380
It's rolled along its longitudinal axis down to the proper gauge.

178
00:16:12,260 --> 00:16:18,740
Every pass, the top roll gets lowered, so you're squeezing that metal through a narrower,

179
00:16:18,741 --> 00:16:20,140
narrower slot.

180
00:16:20,460 --> 00:16:27,460
Just like the raw sheets that become aluminum foil, the metal will get thinner and thinner

181
00:16:28,260 --> 00:16:33,260
and longer and longer until it is about one inch thick.

182
00:16:33,261 --> 00:16:40,260
Saws then cut each flattened plate to various shapes depending on where they'll be positioned

183
00:16:42,100 --> 00:16:47,260
on the wing.

184
00:16:47,261 --> 00:16:53,300
Finally, twin milling heads refine and taper the pieces as necessary by skimming the plate surface.

185
00:16:55,720 --> 00:16:57,977
We use 30-inch diameter cutter.

186
00:16:57,978 --> 00:17:03,000
It will generate this machine pattern on here that looks like swirls.

187
00:17:05,480 --> 00:17:10,740
The completed wing skins are now ready for assembly by Airbus and other aircraft manufacturers.

188
00:17:10,741 --> 00:17:21,980
Flying with anything but aluminum seems almost inconceivable today.

189
00:17:21,981 --> 00:17:27,740
Yet plane-making pioneers fashioned the bodies of their crafts out of fabric-covered wood.

190
00:17:27,741 --> 00:17:32,240
But wood could rot in splinter.

191
00:17:32,241 --> 00:17:38,240
Seeking a more durable material following World War I, German designer Hugo Junkers was

192
00:17:38,241 --> 00:17:45,240
was among the first to build planes sheathed in an aluminum alloy.

193
00:17:46,240 --> 00:17:53,240
In 1935, the Douglas DC-3 and its all-aluminum body ushered in the age of commercial flight.

194
00:17:55,240 --> 00:18:01,240
Sporting longer and larger wings than its predecessors, it could fly higher and faster.

195
00:18:02,240 --> 00:18:06,240
It could also carry more fuel, minimizing refueling stops.

196
00:18:06,241 --> 00:18:13,240
The duration of a coast-to-coast flight fell from an agonizing 26 hours to less than 18.

197
00:18:13,241 --> 00:18:23,240
And today, aluminum carries commercial travelers through the air for more than 800 billion miles every year.

198
00:18:27,240 --> 00:18:33,240
But planes aren't the only form of transportation revolutionized by aluminum.

199
00:18:33,241 --> 00:18:39,640
Each year, the automotive industry uses over 15 billion pounds of aluminum to make our cars.

200
00:18:42,840 --> 00:18:46,960
And with every year, it's using proportionally less and less steel.

201
00:18:48,140 --> 00:18:48,880
Why?

202
00:18:49,760 --> 00:18:51,880
Well, for starters, it saves gas.

203
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:56,920
Aluminum is significantly lighter than steel.

204
00:18:56,921 --> 00:19:03,680
and it gives you somewhere between 30 and 50 percent mass reduction compared to steel.

205
00:19:04,480 --> 00:19:09,180
And the general ratio is if you take 10 percent of the mass out of a vehicle,

206
00:19:09,680 --> 00:19:14,620
you can get up to about 8 percent of improvement in fuel economy.

207
00:19:16,880 --> 00:19:21,760
Automakers prefer aluminum not only because it's the perfect diet bill for steel-heavy cars,

208
00:19:22,160 --> 00:19:25,460
but also because it's easier to melt and to shake.

209
00:19:26,920 --> 00:19:30,920
That's a big plus when it comes to casting the parts for the engine.

210
00:19:30,921 --> 00:19:33,920
That represents much of a car's weight.

211
00:19:35,920 --> 00:19:37,920
What we've done here is take two crucibles.

212
00:19:37,921 --> 00:19:41,920
We've filled one with aluminum and the other one with chunks of steel.

213
00:19:41,921 --> 00:19:48,920
We put both of these crucibles in a furnace and we set the furnace at 800 degrees centigrade.

214
00:19:48,921 --> 00:19:55,920
You can see that the aluminum is melted and the steel is still chunks of steel that are

215
00:19:57,700 --> 00:20:02,087
sitting there, maybe a bit red hot, but nevertheless not melted.

216
00:20:02,088 --> 00:20:04,280
This is one of the most critical

217
00:20:04,281 --> 00:20:10,040
and important attributes of aluminum, that it can melt at low temperatures, so we can

218
00:20:10,041 --> 00:20:13,980
melt it and pour it into cavities to make castings.

219
00:20:13,981 --> 00:20:21,140
While this fluid wonder metal is helping cars lose weight under their hoods, it's also helping

220
00:20:21,141 --> 00:20:23,260
them get their bodies into shape.

221
00:20:23,261 --> 00:20:28,860
It also allows you to make a lot of shapes that you couldn't make otherwise.

222
00:20:28,861 --> 00:20:34,360
One way GM shapes its aluminum parts is with water, in a process known as hydroforming.

223
00:20:34,361 --> 00:20:40,560
The reason we tube hydroform is we're able to form it in any particular shape that we

224
00:20:40,561 --> 00:20:43,680
We need it without welding sections together.

225
00:20:43,681 --> 00:20:50,680
The hydroforming process allows us to make one part and it's proven to be higher durability

226
00:20:50,681 --> 00:20:56,640
than you get out of two pieces welded together.

227
00:20:56,641 --> 00:21:02,040
These water-shaped tubes will become part of the frame of a Corvette.

228
00:21:02,041 --> 00:21:10,160
Once a tube enters the die, high-capacity pumps flood it with up to 100 gallons of water.

229
00:21:10,161 --> 00:21:16,160
The resulting pressure helps the aluminum form into the precise shapes of the die.

230
00:21:16,161 --> 00:21:20,440
It's all over in less than two minutes.

231
00:21:20,441 --> 00:21:27,280
I'm sitting in the Corvette body structure that contains aluminum hydroformed rails.

232
00:21:27,281 --> 00:21:35,660
These aluminum rails replaced steel rails that serve the same function but were heavier.

233
00:21:35,661 --> 00:21:43,200
This enables us to produce a Corvette that is lighter and has better zero to 60 time than you would get with the steel version.

234
00:21:45,820 --> 00:21:49,640
The pressure to shape aluminum car parts isn't restricted to water.

235
00:21:53,720 --> 00:21:58,820
You can also use air with a new technology known as QPF.

236
00:21:58,821 --> 00:22:03,720
It stands for quick plastic forming, which is not plastic.

237
00:22:03,880 --> 00:22:04,920
We're working with aluminum.

238
00:22:04,921 --> 00:22:12,960
but we take aluminum to an elevated temperature state in order to form parts and shapes that we can't normally form.

239
00:22:13,700 --> 00:22:17,700
In this operation, we're making the Cadillac STS trunk lid.

240
00:22:18,480 --> 00:22:21,620
The inner panel is just now coming out of the press fully formed.

241
00:22:22,080 --> 00:22:25,160
What's coming in is the preheated aluminum sheet.

242
00:22:26,260 --> 00:22:34,040
As soon as that die gets closed, we use air pressure to force that aluminum off against that steel die cavity

243
00:22:34,041 --> 00:22:35,700
that gives us our shape.

244
00:22:38,040 --> 00:22:40,900
QPF Technology fashioned aluminum body panels

245
00:22:40,901 --> 00:22:43,420
for GM's fuel cell-powered concept car.

246
00:22:44,880 --> 00:22:45,580
The sequel.

247
00:22:48,200 --> 00:22:51,620
Whether powered by hydrogen, battery, or hybrid engines,

248
00:22:52,280 --> 00:22:54,580
future vehicles must become lighter than ever

249
00:22:54,581 --> 00:22:55,780
to be competitive.

250
00:22:57,920 --> 00:23:00,240
When you put a lot of money into the powertrain,

251
00:23:00,360 --> 00:23:01,800
like you need to do in those vehicles,

252
00:23:01,801 --> 00:23:04,900
it doesn't make sense to pull around a steel cage.

253
00:23:06,820 --> 00:23:10,800
But aluminum is also a big hit in a different kind of cage,

254
00:23:11,140 --> 00:23:12,740
a batting cage.

255
00:23:13,400 --> 00:23:17,120
And one of the secrets to creating a bat that will clean up against the competition

256
00:23:17,121 --> 00:23:21,140
is to give it one outrageously hot bat.

257
00:23:23,820 --> 00:23:30,140
In 1973, a car on the road in America contained an average of 81 pounds of aluminum.

258
00:23:30,141 --> 00:23:37,140
By 2006, the average was 319 pounds.

259
00:23:37,141 --> 00:23:41,140
Aluminum will return on Modern Marvels.

260
00:23:41,141 --> 00:23:46,140
We now return to Aluminum on Modern Marvels.

261
00:23:50,140 --> 00:23:54,140
A wood bat is the foundation of professional baseball

262
00:23:54,141 --> 00:23:59,140
and has been since before the days of the Babe.

263
00:24:00,140 --> 00:24:04,380
To maintain that tradition, pro players can't use anything else.

264
00:24:07,280 --> 00:24:10,200
But aspiring pros aren't required to use wood.

265
00:24:11,900 --> 00:24:18,720
In fact, more than 90% of all bats sold today are made not from wood, but aluminum.

266
00:24:20,440 --> 00:24:22,040
The reasons are obvious.

267
00:24:23,560 --> 00:24:29,740
First, bats made from aluminum are lighter, enabling players to generate more bat speed as they swing.

268
00:24:30,140 --> 00:24:39,300
Unlike wood, aluminum can be strategically balanced along the length of the bat, allowing

269
00:24:39,301 --> 00:24:44,300
it to channel vibrations and transfer energy more efficiently.

270
00:24:44,301 --> 00:24:51,700
As a result, on contact, baseballs fly up to 20 feet farther, and the bat's sweet spot,

271
00:24:51,701 --> 00:24:56,320
the ideal hit zone, is larger.

272
00:24:56,321 --> 00:25:02,700
They debuted at the collegiate level in 1974 and forever altered the nature of the game.

273
00:25:02,701 --> 00:25:08,700
By 1976, the batting averages for NCAA had climbed 30 points.

274
00:25:08,701 --> 00:25:14,740
So there was a big difference in terms of just simply putting the ball in play.

275
00:25:14,741 --> 00:25:19,560
The Anderson Bat Company in Orange County, California, one of the few remaining American

276
00:25:19,561 --> 00:25:29,280
bat makers, crafts 300 aluminum bats every day.

277
00:25:29,281 --> 00:25:34,900
The simple shape of the aluminum bat masks the precise science behind its creation.

278
00:25:34,901 --> 00:25:40,340
The aluminum has the strength to weight ratio that is probably the most advantageous of

279
00:25:40,341 --> 00:25:43,180
any of the metals.

280
00:25:43,181 --> 00:25:47,840
Anderson makes its top of the line bat from an aluminum alloy with a touch of zinc that

281
00:25:47,841 --> 00:25:52,160
It adds toughness and durability.

282
00:25:52,161 --> 00:25:56,160
The process begins with 17-foot long hollow aluminum tubes.

283
00:25:56,161 --> 00:26:05,820
An operator saws the tubes to lengths ranging from 22 to 26 inches, depending on the final

284
00:26:05,821 --> 00:26:10,160
bat model.

285
00:26:10,161 --> 00:26:15,200
Then it's up to a machine called the rotary swagger to reshape each tube into a precisely

286
00:26:15,201 --> 00:26:22,200
molded and balanced bat.

287
00:26:22,201 --> 00:26:26,880
The aluminum fits over a mandrel bar as it enters the swedger between a twin pair of

288
00:26:26,881 --> 00:26:29,920
dies.

289
00:26:29,921 --> 00:26:34,280
As the tapered die halves rotate, they open out.

290
00:26:34,281 --> 00:26:39,860
A series of spinning rollers, positioned around the perimeter, shove the dies back and close

291
00:26:39,861 --> 00:26:42,860
them.

292
00:26:42,861 --> 00:26:49,040
The rotation opens and closes the dies 1,500 times per minute.

293
00:26:49,041 --> 00:26:53,220
As the aluminum feeds into the swagger, it's forced to take the shape of the narrowing

294
00:26:53,221 --> 00:26:57,480
die cavity.

295
00:26:57,481 --> 00:27:02,100
The aluminum needs three passes through the rotary swagger.

296
00:27:02,101 --> 00:27:06,180
Each pass thins the wall and elongates the tube.

297
00:27:06,181 --> 00:27:10,160
The final pass tapers the handle.

298
00:27:10,161 --> 00:27:15,280
This is the cut stock, this is what you saw earlier, and it's 26 inches long.

299
00:27:15,281 --> 00:27:19,720
This one is a first pass, it's the thinning stage of it.

300
00:27:19,721 --> 00:27:25,740
This is a second thinning stage, and you can see that as we go and thin, the tube gets

301
00:27:25,741 --> 00:27:26,940
longer.

302
00:27:26,941 --> 00:27:36,880
And then this is the final pass, and while this is 26, this is 41 inches long.

303
00:27:36,881 --> 00:27:42,800
These bats may now be expertly shaped and balanced, but before they can punish baseballs,

304
00:27:42,801 --> 00:27:46,180
they have to be hardened.

305
00:27:46,181 --> 00:27:54,720
This happens just a few miles away, at a facility where a hot bath waits to heat treat the aluminum.

306
00:27:54,721 --> 00:28:01,380
The bath doesn't hold hot water, but rather sodium nitrate.

307
00:28:01,381 --> 00:28:04,940
Unlike water, the salt solution won't corrode the aluminum.

308
00:28:04,941 --> 00:28:06,998
Heat treat is a two-step process.

309
00:28:06,999 --> 00:28:12,860
An elevated temperature for a certain amount of time and then a rapid cooling called a quench.

310
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:18,531
It's kind of exciting because you go from the salt bath to a water tank.

311
00:28:18,532 --> 00:28:21,300
You have to do it with less than 15 seconds.

312
00:28:22,240 --> 00:28:30,800
The rapid cooling following the elevated temperature soak creates microscopic particles in the aluminum, which strengthen the metal.

313
00:28:30,801 --> 00:28:38,100
After the bats bake in an oven to preserve their hardness and shape, an inspector checks

314
00:28:38,101 --> 00:28:41,440
each one to see if it's a hit or an error.

315
00:28:41,441 --> 00:28:44,800
What we're looking for is a range.

316
00:28:44,801 --> 00:28:48,560
If it's too hard, the aluminum can crack.

317
00:28:48,561 --> 00:28:54,820
And if it's too soft, obviously it will dent.

318
00:28:54,821 --> 00:28:58,820
After the inspection, workers at another facility add the graphics and color.

319
00:28:58,821 --> 00:29:05,340
And workers back at Anderson Bat Company apply the finishing touches.

320
00:29:06,720 --> 00:29:11,320
An end on the handle, a cap, and the grip.

321
00:29:13,360 --> 00:29:16,940
How does this finely crafted aluminum bat compare to one made of wood?

322
00:29:17,240 --> 00:29:21,900
What we're measuring is the velocity of the ball off of the bat from the tee.

323
00:29:22,320 --> 00:29:28,420
And the radar gun actually picks up the very fastest point of the ball between the tee and the gun.

324
00:29:28,820 --> 00:29:30,860
And we're going to start with the wood bat.

325
00:29:33,000 --> 00:29:35,440
Okay, that's 86 miles an hour.

326
00:29:37,580 --> 00:29:38,300
85.

327
00:29:42,800 --> 00:29:44,760
And now the aluminum bat.

328
00:29:46,680 --> 00:29:47,380
91.

329
00:29:51,000 --> 00:29:51,700
91.

330
00:29:52,540 --> 00:29:53,240
Wow.

331
00:29:53,241 --> 00:29:59,440
If, say this point right here is my ideal hit zone, where I'm going to get my best performance,

332
00:29:59,980 --> 00:30:06,140
with an aluminum bat, the size of that hit zone is going to be larger than with a wood bat.

333
00:30:06,440 --> 00:30:10,620
So say the wood bat is going to be the size of a baseball.

334
00:30:11,540 --> 00:30:13,100
Your aluminum bat is going to be double that.

335
00:30:13,140 --> 00:30:14,420
It's going to be the size of two baseballs.

336
00:30:14,700 --> 00:30:18,220
So the chances of me getting better performance when I miss hit a bat

337
00:30:18,221 --> 00:30:20,620
is going to increase as opposed to using a wood bat.

338
00:30:23,180 --> 00:30:27,180
Will aluminum bats ever graduate from college to the pros?

339
00:30:27,181 --> 00:30:30,180
Don't hold your breath.

340
00:30:30,181 --> 00:30:34,180
Baseball is a very traditional sport, and wood, the crack of the bat,

341
00:30:34,181 --> 00:30:38,180
and all of that is very much a part of the fabric of baseball.

342
00:30:38,181 --> 00:30:43,180
At the same time, if you were to put an aluminum bat in the hands of professional players,

343
00:30:43,181 --> 00:30:47,180
you would have to change all of the records. They'd have to be asterisk.

344
00:30:47,181 --> 00:30:52,180
Ball players aren't the only ones reaching new heights with aluminum.

345
00:30:52,181 --> 00:30:59,180
NASA is using it to gaze into the deepest reaches of the universe.

346
00:30:59,181 --> 00:31:08,180
At the point of contact, an aluminum bat generates up to four tons of force on a baseball thrown at 90 miles per hour.

347
00:31:08,181 --> 00:31:13,180
Aluminum will return on Modern Marvels.

348
00:31:13,181 --> 00:31:19,180
We now return to Aluminum on Modern Marvels.

349
00:31:19,181 --> 00:31:31,380
In 2016, a new telescope called the Giant Magellan, the world's largest, will begin peering skyward from an observatory in Chile.

350
00:31:34,260 --> 00:31:42,000
A cluster of seven mirrors more than 80 feet in diameter will redirect so much light from the heavens to astronomers' eyes,

351
00:31:42,720 --> 00:31:47,900
it will produce images up to 10 times sharper than the Hubble Space Telescope.

352
00:31:49,180 --> 00:31:55,080
It will allow astronomers to study newly discovered black holes, stars and galaxies.

353
00:31:59,140 --> 00:32:05,460
But the giant Magellan wouldn't see anything without aluminum coating each mirror.

354
00:32:07,400 --> 00:32:13,660
Your mirror at home uses a layer of silver, and it's actually slightly more reflective than aluminum.

355
00:32:13,661 --> 00:32:20,660
But astronomers use aluminum for their telescopes because it's more durable and less expensive

356
00:32:21,260 --> 00:32:22,271
to maintain.

357
00:32:22,272 --> 00:32:28,340
Making aluminum-coated mirrors for telescopes isn't as simple as dipping

358
00:32:28,341 --> 00:32:32,908
a brush in buckets of aluminum paint and slapping it on glass.

359
00:32:32,909 --> 00:32:35,340
Just ask the scientists at NASA's

360
00:32:36,620 --> 00:32:43,620
Goddard Space Flight Center in Maryland. The setting for the transformation of glass

361
00:32:43,621 --> 00:32:46,980
into mirror is a vacuum chamber.

362
00:32:46,981 --> 00:32:51,600
The process of coating the mirror

363
00:32:51,601 --> 00:32:55,000
demands that the environment not be contaminated.

364
00:32:55,001 --> 00:32:59,640
We actually evacuate most of the air out of the chamber.

365
00:32:59,641 --> 00:33:01,800
So the cleaner the chamber is, the cleaner

366
00:33:01,801 --> 00:33:04,680
the coating is going to be.

367
00:33:04,681 --> 00:33:08,020
With the glass in place, it's time to add the aluminum.

368
00:33:08,021 --> 00:33:13,580
These staples are 99.999 pure aluminum.

369
00:33:13,581 --> 00:33:17,360
We use these as the base for the aluminum coatings.

370
00:33:17,980 --> 00:33:23,560
We put them by hand on the tungsten filaments to prepare for the coating process.

371
00:33:24,340 --> 00:33:30,580
The lid lowers, sealing the chamber, and a pump removes virtually all the air.

372
00:33:34,400 --> 00:33:41,580
An electric current heats the filaments, melting the staples and removing any lingering impurities in the aluminum.

373
00:33:41,581 --> 00:33:46,260
Once the aluminum is melted, this is the way it looks on the filament.

374
00:33:46,660 --> 00:33:54,980
So you can see that the aluminum is no longer a hard staple, but it's actually wetted along the coil of the filament.

375
00:33:56,460 --> 00:34:00,880
Next, a second stronger electric current passes through the filaments.

376
00:34:02,360 --> 00:34:05,200
In a blinding flash, the aluminum vaporizes.

377
00:34:05,660 --> 00:34:10,260
The hot aluminum gas rises and condenses on the cooler glass surface.

378
00:34:11,580 --> 00:34:16,240
The deposited layer is 1,500 times thinner than a human hair.

379
00:34:19,380 --> 00:34:23,840
But as shiny as the aluminum coating is, it faces a host of enemies.

380
00:34:24,960 --> 00:34:30,800
Over time, moisture and pollen and bugs and things like that will get on the optical surfaces

381
00:34:30,801 --> 00:34:35,060
and they'll start etching into the aluminum and they degrade the coating over time.

382
00:34:35,640 --> 00:34:40,280
The only way to restore an aluminum-coated mirror is to remove the corrupted coating

383
00:34:40,281 --> 00:34:41,940
and replace it with a fresh one.

384
00:34:43,960 --> 00:34:46,180
It's a major event for observatories

385
00:34:46,181 --> 00:34:49,300
and a necessity about every two years.

386
00:34:51,500 --> 00:34:53,900
First, technicians must remove the mirror

387
00:34:53,901 --> 00:34:55,140
from the telescope's housing.

388
00:34:58,020 --> 00:35:00,540
Stripping off the old coating starts with soap and water.

389
00:35:03,280 --> 00:35:05,740
Chemical solvents then eat away the aluminum coating

390
00:35:05,741 --> 00:35:08,120
to reveal the underlying glass base.

391
00:35:10,280 --> 00:35:14,320
Next, paper towels are used to clean and dry the glass,

392
00:35:14,780 --> 00:35:19,240
before a vacuum chamber lowers into place and deposits the shiny aluminum.

393
00:35:22,600 --> 00:35:27,940
Bugs and dirt are to worry for the orbiting 95-inch aluminum-coated mirror on the Hubble.

394
00:35:29,900 --> 00:35:33,660
But NASA engineers have to guard it against an entirely different threat.

395
00:35:34,700 --> 00:35:38,040
You go up in space and you have extreme differentials.

396
00:35:38,041 --> 00:35:44,900
If it's facing the sun, if it's nighttime, you're talking hundreds of degrees in variation.

397
00:35:45,380 --> 00:35:48,940
And that'll change the contour of the mirror.

398
00:35:50,680 --> 00:35:56,900
Trying to lick the problem, NASA scientists have developed a new kind of aluminum mirror, without a glass base.

399
00:35:58,340 --> 00:36:06,020
You can use the aluminum to make your mirror and the mounting structure all out of the same material.

400
00:36:06,021 --> 00:36:15,560
If the temperature changes, then your mirror and your mounting structure then shrinks or expands by the same amount.

401
00:36:15,760 --> 00:36:22,740
So you don't get distortions and stresses that will twist the mirror and destroy its imaging properties.

402
00:36:23,820 --> 00:36:30,240
The challenge for NASA's engineers is to turn pure aluminum disks like this one into mirrors.

403
00:36:30,241 --> 00:36:36,420
pulling that off requires getting rid of the rough surface

404
00:36:36,421 --> 00:36:41,480
a diamond tipped lathe is the answer

405
00:36:41,481 --> 00:36:49,980
it will dig in just below the surface and in a uniform slice begin to smooth the aluminum to

406
00:36:49,981 --> 00:36:56,980
a natural shine.

407
00:36:57,760 --> 00:37:03,100
During the procedure, a paint thinner solution sprays away debris.

408
00:37:03,101 --> 00:37:09,860
It also cools the diamond tip, which heats up as it carves into the metal.

409
00:37:09,861 --> 00:37:14,100
And after just two minutes,

410
00:37:14,101 --> 00:37:18,980
a final polishing treatment will smooth out leftover microscopic imperfections.

411
00:37:18,981 --> 00:37:22,480
NASA scientists speculate that pure aluminum mirrors like this

412
00:37:22,481 --> 00:37:26,380
could someday replace the more common aluminum-coated glass mirrors.

413
00:37:30,400 --> 00:37:34,280
Of course, NASA's love affair with aluminum isn't just limited to mirrors.

414
00:37:36,220 --> 00:37:40,040
Anything you want to lift off the surface of the Earth, you're fighting gravity.

415
00:37:40,041 --> 00:37:43,440
So you want to look for materials that are very lightweight and strong

416
00:37:43,441 --> 00:37:46,020
and take advantage of other properties.

417
00:37:46,021 --> 00:37:53,020
Reducing the mass to take it to orbit is a big deal.

418
00:37:53,021 --> 00:37:56,020
Every pound, every ounce counts.

419
00:37:56,021 --> 00:38:01,020
So aluminum has a perfect combination of many of the characteristics we look for.

420
00:38:01,021 --> 00:38:03,020
It is very lightweight.

421
00:38:03,021 --> 00:38:07,020
It's malleable, meaning that you can work with it easier.

422
00:38:07,021 --> 00:38:09,020
It doesn't corrode.

423
00:38:09,021 --> 00:38:15,020
If you look around anywhere in NASA, you will see it being applied in just about everything we do.

424
00:38:15,021 --> 00:38:20,020
And it's been that way for over five decades.

425
00:38:20,021 --> 00:38:26,020
It has been one of those materials that actually have made many of the things that we're doing now possible.

426
00:38:26,021 --> 00:38:32,020
As aluminum continues to help us explore new worlds,

427
00:38:32,021 --> 00:38:38,020
it may also prevent terrorist bombings from leveling our buildings.

428
00:38:38,021 --> 00:38:43,020
This spongy new wonder could save your life.

429
00:38:43,021 --> 00:38:47,220
The space shuttle is not only made from aluminum, but also fueled by it.

430
00:38:48,380 --> 00:38:52,280
Each of its twin rocket boosters requires one million pounds of fuel,

431
00:38:53,420 --> 00:38:56,360
16% of which is solid aluminum powder.

432
00:38:57,980 --> 00:39:00,900
Aluminum will return on Modern Marvels.

433
00:39:03,500 --> 00:39:06,980
We now return to Aluminum on Modern Marvels.

434
00:39:06,981 --> 00:39:16,080
1983 Beirut, the United States Embassy Building.

435
00:39:18,740 --> 00:39:23,820
1995 Oklahoma City, the Alfred P. Murrah Federal Building.

436
00:39:25,440 --> 00:39:29,340
1998, the United States Embassies in East Africa.

437
00:39:29,341 --> 00:39:37,180
The structures in these attacks prove no match against a devastating explosion unleashed by a powerful bomb.

438
00:39:38,000 --> 00:39:43,460
But these buildings may have fared better had they been clad in an innovative form of aluminum

439
00:39:43,461 --> 00:39:46,420
produced by Canada's CIMAT technologies.

440
00:39:47,340 --> 00:39:48,900
They call it aluminum foam.

441
00:39:50,520 --> 00:39:54,460
Its unique sponge-like structure could prove to be a lifesaver around the globe.

442
00:39:59,340 --> 00:40:03,740
The foam basically absorbs the shockwave so that there isn't such a severe impact.

443
00:40:04,240 --> 00:40:08,660
And so if you had blast-protected the concrete column supporting the structure,

444
00:40:09,060 --> 00:40:12,980
there's an excellent chance that the buildings wouldn't have collapsed and a lot of lives would have been saved.

445
00:40:16,080 --> 00:40:19,020
Aluminum foam's key ingredient is nothing more than air.

446
00:40:20,540 --> 00:40:24,600
But the real trick to making it isn't injecting air bubbles into the aluminum.

447
00:40:26,280 --> 00:40:29,060
It's keeping the bubbles intact once they're inside.

448
00:40:29,340 --> 00:40:36,340
Our material is molten aluminum with ceramic particles in it.

449
00:40:36,341 --> 00:40:40,340
Those particles stabilize the bubbles, in other words, stop them from popping.

450
00:40:40,341 --> 00:40:46,340
A good example of this is if you've ever tried to use dried cocoa powder and mix it into milk.

451
00:40:46,341 --> 00:40:52,340
And as you're doing it, you see this froth forming on the surface with the dried cocoa on the bubbles.

452
00:40:52,341 --> 00:40:55,340
The bubbles are stable.

453
00:40:55,341 --> 00:41:00,240
Cymat begins its production process by melting down aluminum bars already

454
00:41:00,241 --> 00:41:05,160
containing the ceramic particles. The furnace holding materials spills a

455
00:41:05,161 --> 00:41:12,220
scalding stream into a channel leading to a receptacle called the foaming box.

456
00:41:12,840 --> 00:41:21,740
Now it's time for the all-important air. Inside the foaming box a nozzle injects

457
00:41:21,741 --> 00:41:24,260
air creating bubbles in the molten concoction.

458
00:41:27,540 --> 00:41:31,040
More air creates a less dense and lighter final product.

